      <h1><span>WORLDEOLOGY</span><span>LAB</span></h1>
      <p>Cognitive AI</p>
    </header>

    <article class="cognitive-manifesto" aria-label="Cognitive AI research statement">
      <p>We have spent a long time teaching machines to answer questions, or to carry out tasks.</p>

      <p>A larger question has only just surfaced: what did the machine understand when it received an instruction? As reasoning took shape, could it notice its own deviation? And once an output left the screen and entered a person’s life, where did it push the world?</p>

      <p>Language and scenes are only the part of cognition that briefly rises above the surface. Below it lie guesses about another person’s intentions, plans that weigh competing interests, and countless reasoning paths converging under uncertainty. Beyond the surface, action participates in reality. It changes a decision, a relationship, even a future path that otherwise would never have existed.</p>

      <p class="manifest-emphasis">Cognitive AI studies this entire process.</p>

      <p>It expands the basic unit of intelligence from a single output to a cognitive trajectory: understanding minds before action, examining cognition during action, tracing and bearing the consequences after action, and continuing to learn and generalize across events.</p>

      <p>Future AI will not remain inside a prompt box forever, passively waiting for a question and returning an answer. It will enter sustained collaboration, education, companionship, and decision-making. Then, the correctness of a single response will no longer be enough. A system must know whom it is facing, how a judgment is taking shape, and what its own action will leave behind over time.</p>

      <h3>Before the answer: understand another mind.</h3>
      <p>Human interaction is never transparent. People hint, withhold, and test. Their real intentions may hide behind an ordinary action. Before it speaks, Cognitive AI confronts these invisible states—inferring from context, relationships, and behavior what another person may believe, what they may need, and which boundaries must not be crossed.</p>

      <h3>Inside the answer: observe and examine cognition.</h3>
      <p>Cognitive AI turns generation from a line that can only move forward into a path that can be seen, inspected, and retraced. When reasoning begins to drift, the system need not wait until the error has been fully stated and then explain it. It can find where the deviation began, return, and choose again.</p>

      <h3>After the answer: model how action changes the world.</h3>
      <p>Once an action enters reality, it is no longer merely a physical interaction. A moment of comfort may change another person’s beliefs; one suggestion may initiate a major event. Every step can appear reasonable while the long-term outcome slowly moves in the opposite direction. Cognitive AI is concerned with the world that continues to grow after an action.</p>

      <p>We intend to establish a new field: an AI research program that looks not only at the surface of an answer, but also at the cognitive process from which it emerged and the trajectory it leaves after passing through reality. Understanding, examination, and consequence thus become objects that can be modeled, trained, evaluated, and optimized.</p>

      <p class="manifest-ending">The answer is a point in the age of AI. Cognition begins here—and continues into the future.</p>
    </article>

